==English==
{{wikipedia|steaming|mul=steaming (disambiguation)}}

===Etymology===
{{surf|en|steam|-ing}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈstiː.mɪŋ/|a=UK}}
* {{rhymes|en|iːmɪŋ|s=2}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# The action of [[steam]] on something.
#: {{ux|en|'''Steaming''' is fine for this fabric, as long as you keep the iron moving.}}
# The method of [[cooking]] by [[immersion]] in steam.
#: {{cot|en|sweating#Noun|[[sauté]]ing|boiling#Noun|frying#Noun}}
#: {{ux|en|Their favorite way to cook clams is '''steaming'''.}}
# The act or process of [[seafaring]] under [[steam power]].
#: {{ux|en|'''Steaming''' was their preferred way to travel until airliners came along.}}
# The raising of steam by a [[steam locomotive]] etc; the maintenance of a [[head of steam]] during operation; {{lb|en|metonymically}} operation under load.
#: {{hypo|en|steaming up}}
#: {{ux|en|Two 4-4-0s were '''steaming''' through the scenic foothills, giving rides to tourists.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1947|month=January and February|author=Cecil J. Allen|title=British Locomotive Practice and Performance|journal=Railway Magazine|page=39|text=The response to firing is remarkable, and though the 280 lb. pressure seems more in the nature of a reserve for emergencies than a continuous working figure, even with the present-day apology for coal it seldom fell below 250 lb., and could always be brought up to blowing-off point with little difficulty. To this liveliness of '''steaming''' the thermic syphons are doubtless the chief contributory.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1961|month=February|author='Balmore'|title=Driving and firing modern French steam locomotives - Part One|journal=Trains Illustrated|page=109|text=The perfect '''steaming''' and beautiful mechanical condition of the Pacific were immediately apparent.}}
# {{lb|en|UK|informal}} A form of [[robbery]] in which a large [[gang]] moves swiftly and violently through a bus or train.

